A||||8||Cooter||3.4 conversion questions||||5||
Z||000000||Cooter||12-11-1999||10:17 PM||||Because my new 88 GT has well over 170,000 miles on it, I am going to replace the engine soon. I have found a 95 camaro 3.4 v6 for a reasonable price. Questions:<BR>1.) My current getrag 5 speed is not in good shape (grinds BAD going into 3rd and 5th), but I have a good Isuzu 5 sp. Which clutch disk and pressure plate will I need?<BR>2.) Can I use the flywheel from the 3.4 since it is a 5 speed in the camaro?<BR>3.) Why must the starter be moved from one side to the other on the 3.4? What does it interfere with? Can a hi-torque "mini" starter be used in the stock location?<BR>4.) What kind of performance can I expect?||||reg||5
Z||000001||fiero56||12-11-1999||10:41 PM||||You should use the clutch designed for the tranny you are going to use. <BR>You may just need to replace a syncro in the Getrag, if you wanted to use it.  Is that Izuzu you want to use from a 4-cyl? I'd ask Ed Parks what transmission he recommends you use with that engine.<BR>I believe the starter problem has to do with the cradle.  I'm sure batboy would know exaclty though.<BR>I don't know what torque or horsepower those engines are, but I suspect you'd notice more "passing" power. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||terryk||12-12-1999||12:16 AM||||This has some good information. It all applies to the Fiero eventhough it is an X-11.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.home.net/sprigint/x11p/engine/engine.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.home.net/sprigint/x11p/engine/engine.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||12-12-1999||01:43 AM|||| <A HREF="http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/e-conv.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/e-conv.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||12-12-1999||11:04 AM||||Thanks to all.||||reg||1
Z||000005||olympic||12-12-1999||02:45 PM||||Ummmmm, I'd be careful with that Isuzu unit. American Custom Engineering says that it is only good up to 120 lbs-ft of torque or so. The getrag can handle about twice that. The engines you mention all produce over 200 lbs-ft of torque.||||reg||4
Z||000006||Raydar||12-12-1999||03:23 PM||||I believe the reasoning behind using the Isuzu with the V-6 is that, if it breaks, they are plentiful and relatively inexpensive. Probably after you've replaced 1 or 2 of them, it'll get "routine".   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I suspect the Izuzu will last if you don't drive it too hard. (No smoky-tire burnouts or speed shifting.)  <BR>The guy in Montgomery, with the tricked out Formula, has built a 3.4/Isuzu 88 coupe. Check here... <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/NewProject.html]" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/NewProject.html]</A> <p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-12-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||12-12-1999||04:44 PM||||I checked while ago, the 3.4 was gone! That really stinks. I noticed the same yard has several GM mini-vans. I will post a new thread about using the 3.1 liter from one of them.||||reg||8
Z||000008||pherder||12-12-1999||07:23 PM||||You HAVE to mount the starter on the front side of the motor since there is a very needed piece of equipment in the way on the rear side ... The right axle!!!  No room for anything else there.<P>BTW, 90-93 Lumina APV (and it's sisters from Pontiac and Olds) have the cast iron head 3.1 motors.  I know they have them in 1993 since I have one in the shop.  As for the earliest year...1990?||||reg||4
